\title[Combinatorics of KL-regularized alignment]{A Combinatorial Theory of KL-Regularized Alignment on the Boolean Lattice}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop an exact, finitary theory of the KL-regularized reward-maximization objective
$J(q) = \E_q[r] - \beta\,\KL(q\|p)$ --- the objective underlying reinforcement learning
from human or symbolic feedback --- on response spaces with combinatorial structure.
Two structures are treated. First, \emph{product spaces}: when the reference policy is a
product measure and the reward is additive across coordinates, the partition function is
multiplicative, the optimal policy is again a product, and divergence, objective and
optimal value are all additive; for $n$ i.i.d.\ coordinates this yields exact linear
scaling laws for alignment value, information drift, and the cost of a pretraining
mix-in term. Second, the \emph{Boolean lattice} $2^{[n]}$ of feature sets under a uniform
reference: for the counting reward $r(S) = a|S|$ every object of the theory has a closed
form. The partition function is a binomial-theorem evaluation
$Z = ((1+e^{a/\beta})/2)^n$; the optimal policy is exactly a product of i.i.d.\
$\Ber(\sigma(a/\beta))$ features with $\sigma$ the logistic function; the reward statistic
$|S|$ is exactly $\mathrm{Binomial}(n,\sigma(a/\beta))$ with mean $n\sigma(a/\beta)$; the
entropy is $n H(\sigma(a/\beta))$ and the drift is
$n\bigl((a/\beta)\sigma(a/\beta) - \log\frac{1+e^{a/\beta}}{2}\bigr)$. Log-concavity of
the binomial coefficients implies that the induced law of the reward statistic is
log-concave, hence unimodal, ruling out bimodal degeneration. A Bernoulli-inequality
argument gives the quantitative mode-collapse bound $\pi(\text{argmax}) \ge 1 - ne^{-a/\beta}$,
and both achieved reward and collapse mass are strictly antitone in the regularization
strength. For the non-additive rewards arising from conjunctive symbolic rules we identify
\emph{supermodularity} as the governing hypothesis: the optimal policy is then
log-supermodular, so by the Fortuin--Kasteleyn--Ginibre inequality any two increasing
observables are positively correlated, and by Holley's inequality a monotone reward makes
the optimal policy stochastically dominate the reference. The technical engine throughout
is enumerative and lattice combinatorics rather than analysis.
\end{abstract}

